{"about":"0b14385d18dbb54744c46bd88d961c2cf563cc0e20d78e2419a4362748cd7e4b","body":"By defining the preferred theory solely as the 'least refuted option,' the conjecture ignores that corroboration reports on the severity of tests passed, not just the absence of failure. Reducing the preference to a lack of refutation fails to explain why a theory that has survived a thousand severe tests is practically preferred over a new, untested theory that is also technically 'unrefuted.' The conjecture offers no non-inductive mechanism to weight the history of survival against the blank slate of a new hypothesis.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The definition of corroboration as merely negative"}